      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm involves repeated division and taking remainders until we reach a remainder of zero. The last non-zero remainder is the GCD.\n\n1. Divide 1407 by 903 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   1407 \\div 903 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 1407 - 903 \\cdot 1 = 504.\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   1407 = 903 \\cdot 1 + 504.\n   \\]\n\n2. Now, divide 903 by 504 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   903 \\div 504 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 903 - 504 \\cdot 1 = 405.\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   903 = 504 \\cdot 1 + 405.\n   \\]\n\n3. Next, divide 504 by 405 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   504 \\div 405 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 504 - 405 \\cdot 1 = 99.\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   504 = 405 \\cdot 1 + 99.\n   \\]\n\n4. Then, divide 405 by 99 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   405 \\div 99 = 4 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 405 - 99 \\cdot 4 = 0.\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   405 = 99 \\cdot 4 + 0.\n   \\]\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the last non-zero remainder is 99. Therefore, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is \\(\\boxed{99}\\).",
